    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Grazing organisms can negatively affect giant kelp (Macrocystis pyrifera) individuals and ecosystems by consuming large amounts of biomass. M. pyrifera, however, serves important ecosystem functions, providing food, habitat, shelter, and nursing grounds for a suite of organisms. Thus, it is crucial to understand M. pyrifera’s mechanisms of defense in order to predict how M. pyrifera responds to heavy grazing events and how M. pyrifera protects itself on a day-to-day basis. Little is known about the distribution of M. pyrifera’s chemical defense compounds (polyphenols) across life stages and within a blade. To test this, M. pyrifera blades were subjected to grazing by Chlorostoma brunnea. This was repeated for juvenile, adult, and apical blades. Polyphenol levels of each blade were tested and compared. Polyphenol concentrations were found to be highest in apical blades, lowest in juvenile blades, and moderate in adult blades. Within juvenile blades, the tip contained the lowest concentration of polyphenols, whereas the base contained the highest. Polyphenol distributions were found to align with distributions of compounds and biological processes that may enhance the individual’s fitness.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
